name macro_ground
describe Camera 30 cm above the ground looking down at 45 degrees, holding still. The close-up look shot: at 7.8 mm voxels this is where individual cubes are several pixels across and the John Lin look either reads or does not. Post is left on because the look INCLUDES the tone mapping.
backend both

# TAA off for every scenario that compares a capture against a golden. Measured, not assumed:
# with TAA on, vk-vs-vk moves 0.996% of pixels at this pose against a 1.5% gate, and with it off
# 0.341% -- see dev/harness/src/image_compare.hpp for the full four-case table.
option --no-taa

# Prompt 007 goal 344: --no-wind, and it is a CAPTURE-REPRODUCIBILITY requirement rather than a
# preference. Goals 333/335/338 put moving content in every frame -- a ground shimmer, swaying trees
# and grass -- driven by an animation clock that is the WALL CLOCK by default, so a golden of this
# pose stopped being reproducible. `--anim-step` makes the clock deterministic per FRAME and that is
# not enough on its own, because `capture end` lands on a different frame index between runs (goal
# 337 records that limit). Killing the wind for a golden-comparing capture is what the project
# already does for every wind A/B -- goal 332's own 0.0012% number was measured that way -- and it
# leaves the golden measuring everything else: terrain, tree shape, grass geometry, shading, LOD.
option --no-wind

# This is a CAMERA shot, not a body test: 30 cm is where the eye goes, and a 1.7 m body standing
# on the ground cannot put its eye there. Under the walk default the pose buried the body 1.40 m
# and every tick of the hold reported it. --fly holds the pose against gravity; --noclip is what
# makes the pose legal at all. Measured before the flags: 121 inside-solid ticks, 121 walk
# violations, from a scenario in which nothing moves.
option --fly
option --noclip

pose_ground 48,0 -90 -45 0.3
wait 2

capture end macro_ground

assert frames > 0
assert contrast_percent > 6

# ------------------------------------------------- Prompt 005 goal 291: the ALIASING gate
# A DIFFERENT question from contrast_percent above, and the two move in opposite directions:
# contrast_percent asks "is there texture", moire_ratio asks "how much of that texture is
# energy piled against the pixel Nyquist limit rather than spread over resolvable
# frequencies". A deliberate stipple raises the first and leaves the second alone; aliasing
# raises both. Gate on both or a change that trades one for the other passes unnoticed.
#
# CALIBRATED, and this metric is far tighter than the GPU-time gate above because it is a
# DETERMINISTIC IMAGE MEASUREMENT rather than a clock reading -- no boost state, no
# contention. Three consecutive runs:
#   measured 1.670, spread under 0.05%
#   with --no-filter-albedo (goal 278 off): 2.367
# The gate is 2.0 -- about 1.2x the measured value, and it FAILS at 2.367, which is
# goal 291's own falsification and was verified by running it rather than assumed.
assert moire_ratio < 2.0
